# Why Are Orders Getting Bad Reviews? A Delivery Analysis of 96,000 E-Commerce Orders

## The business query
An e-commerce marketplace experiencing stagnant growth needs to identify the primary cause : sellers, delivery, or customers. **Which domain requires immediate action from the operations manager?**

## Answer
**Late deliveries are the problems, and Rio de Janeiro (RJ) is the primary region causing problem.**
- Orders that arrive after the promised date average **2.57 stars**, against **4.29** for on-time orders.
- **8.1%** of orders arrive late.
- RJ has a **13.5%** late rate against 5.9% in Sao Paulo. It accounts for **21%** of all late orders.
- Bringing RJ to São Paulo's rate would remove about **940 late orders (12% of the total)**.
- The delay happens in **carrier transit**, not at the sellers.

![Late vs on-time](chart1_late_vs_ontime.png)
![Late orders by state](chart3_late_orders_by_state.png)

## Data
Olist Brazilian E-Commerce dataset (Kaggle): 99,441 orders, 8 tables.
Download: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/olistbr/brazilian-ecommerce
**Unit of analysis:** headline results are per order (96,476 delivered orders). The seller analysis is per order item (110,196 rows), because one order can include several sellers.

## Method
1. Explored and evaluated 8 rational tables and found data issues: 551 duplicate reviews, and `customer_id` is one per order (so repeat-customer analysis needs `customer_unique_id`).
2. Merged orders, customers, reviews and order items using pandas, checking row counts after every merge.
3. Tested speed vs reviews, then sellers, then geography, then late vs promised date.
4. Split delivery time into seller handling and carrier transit.

## Findings
**1. Missing guaranteed delivery dates drops ratings by 1.73 stars (3x worse than general slow delivery)** Correlation of delivery days and score is -0.30, but missing the promised date costs 1.73 stars. Setting longer estimated dates preserve customer satisfaction even with 20+ day wait times.
**2. Sellers are not the main problem.** Only 14 of 681 sellers with 30+ orders average over 20 days, and they handle 2.3% of items.
**3. RJ's delay is in transit.** Seller handling is about 2.8 days everywhere. RJ transit is 11.3 days vs 5.1 in SP.
**4. RJ has a tail of very slow orders.** Its median delivery (11 days) is close to MG's (10), but its slowest 10% take 28+ days against 20.

## Recommendations
1. Audit the logistics partner responsible for routes for Rio de Janeiro RJ. RJ has late delivery rate 13.5% as compared to 5.9% in Sao Paulo SP. and the extra delay is in carrier transit i-e 11.3 vs 5.1 days, not at the sellers. Bringing RJ to Sao Paulo’s rate would eliminate about 940 delayed orders, 12% of all delayed orders. Track RJ's monthly late delivery rate to measure operational progress.
2. Review delivery date estimate for RJ. Customers care about missed promised date not just total transit days. On time orders rate 4.29 stars and late orders rate 2.57 stars. So an order that is slow but arrives by the promised date does not hurt the review. Identify who sets promised dates for RJ the platform or the seller. Then add extra days only for RJ orders that look risky, not for all of them. **Trade off**: Longer delivery estimate dates might lower sales so monitor total order volume carefully.**How to measure it:** RJ's late rate, average review score and number of orders each month
3. Proactively contact at risk customers and offer voucher. Late shipment lowers review scores by 1.73 stars. Since delays in RJ happen mainly during carrier transit , where the seller has no control. So the **platform** should send the message, because it can see the tracking data. **Action** Automatically message customers when an RJ order is delayed (still undelivered at day 20) , apologise and offer a discount voucher for their next purchase. **Testing** : Run an A/B testing by sending messages / vouchers to half of the at risk customers and compare review scores against the un messaged half. Limit vouchers strictly to high risk orders to control cost.

## Limitations
- No carrier names or routes in the data, so the cause of slow transit can't be identified.
- Late orders correlate with low scores; causation isn't proven (damaged or wrong items may also play a part).
- Retention (do late orders reduce repeat purchases?) is a next step.

## Problems I hit and how I solved them
1. When I merged review table with orders the row count increased from 99,441 to 99,992 . I checked if there are duplicate entries and I found 551 duplicate order_ids. So I only kept the latest review for each order.  After this fix the row count returned to 99,441.
2. When I explored the customers table, I found two customer ID columns. customer_id had 99,441 unique values and customer_unique_id had 96,096. That is about 3,300 fewer unique people than orders, so only about 3% of orders come from returning customers. I figured out that every time a customer places an order, they get a new customer_id, while customer_unique_id stays the same for the same person. For any retention or repeat-customer analysis I would use customer_unique_id, because with customer_id every customer would look like a one-time buyer.
3. When I merged order_items, I noticed that an order with multiple products is split into several rows: an order with 3 products appeared as 3 rows. This counted multi-item orders more than once and inflated my late counts. I removed the duplicates and kept one row per order. The late rate moved from 7.9% to 8.1%, so the conclusions didn’t change. I now report late rate, review scores and state comparison per order (96,476 delivered orders), and the seller analysis per order item (110,196 rows), because one order can include several sellers.
## Tools
Python (pandas, matplotlib), PyCharm